You are checking the flows described by a static taint-analysis configuration, not doing a free-form audit.

Tai-e, a whole-program static taint analyzer, is run on this project with exactly the inputs below: the entry program it starts from and its taint policy files (Tai-e YAML: sources introduce attacker-controlled data, sinks are the dangerous arguments, transfers propagate taint between values). You receive the same configuration the analyzer receives.

Entry program `SparkEntry.java` (the analysis starts at its `main` method):
```java
import java.io.IOException;
import spark.resource.ClassPathResource;

/** Models an untrusted resource path at the library boundary, not a full HTTP server. */
public class SparkEntry {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        String path = System.getenv("CWE_BENCH_PATH");
        ClassPathResource resource = new ClassPathResource(path);
        resource.getInputStream();
    }
}
```

